  "worked_solution": "We note that $v=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $6v,\\,{v}^{2},$ and $2{v}^{2}.$ First, we find the denominators' least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$). We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(6v,{v}^{2},2{v}^{2})=6{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6{v}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v} & =\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6{v}^{2}(\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v}) & =6{v}^{2}(\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}}) \\\\ 6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{6v} & =6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-6v\\cdot v\\cdot \\frac{7}{6v} & =3\\cdot 2{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6\\cdot \\cancel{{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{\\cancel{{v}^{2}}}-\\cancel{6v}\\cdot v\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\cancel{6v}} & =3\\cdot \\cancel{2{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{2{v}^{2}}} \\\\ 6(v+4)-v\\cdot (7) & =3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 6(v+4)-7v & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $v,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(v+4)-7v & =15 \\\\ 6v+24-7v & =15 \\\\ -v+24 & =15 \\\\ -v & =-9 \\\\ v & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $v=0$ as a solution. But since $v=9\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $v=9.$",
